 "avoidBefore": "upper",
 "energy": "Low energy demand: dozens of short static holds over a long day, so shoulder girdle endurance and an easy aerobic base matter more than fitness for sprinting.",
 "summary": "Archery asks the shoulders to hold a heavy draw steady, shot after shot, and shoulder pain is very common. The plan builds rotator cuff and scapular endurance on both sides, a stable trunk and stance, and grows arrow volume gradually.",
 "evidence": [
  {
   "claim": "In 156 competitive archers, 54% had shoulder pain at the time of the survey and 83% had had it at some point; past shoulder pain was the main linked factor.",
   "source": "Grover & Sinha 2017, Asian Journal of Sports Medicine",
   "url": "https://brieflands.com/articles/asjsm-13338"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Archers who train only one side showed more rotation, mainly internal rotation, on the pull-arm shoulder than archers who train both sides; the authors suggest bilateral training to limit this.",
   "source": "Schmitt, Vogt, Wilke & Niederer 2021, BMC Sports Science, Medicine and Rehabilitation",
   "url": "https://doaj.org/article/49de7c94863d4e259a63b45f564cbbf9"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In US emergency department data, about two thirds of archery injuries in men were to the fingers, mostly cuts, pointing to technique and equipment as the main fixes for acute injury.",
   "source": "Konda et al. 2023, Orthopedic Reviews",
   "url": "https://orthopedicreviews.openmedicalpublishing.org/article/88933-archery-related-musculoskeletal-injuries-an-epidemiological-study-revealing-injury-sites-risk-factors-and-implications-for-prevention"
  }
 ],
